          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is that enough to need something preventive?",
          "answer": "I'm not sure what \"that\" refers to. I'm assuming you mean the six migraines a month you mentioned. If you meant a different reading, test result, or symptom, please tell me what it is and which condition you're asking about.\n\nUnder the way migraine is grouped in the National headache society guidance, preventive treatment is usually offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not enough. The guidance describes the usual threshold as **four or more migraine days per month with some disability**. Six a month is above that frequency. Disability matters too, though. The guidance also allows preventive treatment with fewer attacks if they are severely disabling, if acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or if the person prefers it. (Source: *Migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits*.)\n\nMore generally, clinicians compare a finding with established thresholds and then estimate overall risk. The General clinical reference on hypertension describes the same approach: lifestyle measures apply to everyone, and drug treatment is added according to the level of risk. Exact thresholds and targets are set by the relevant guidelines, which the corpus covers only in outline. A clinician weighs these with the person's full situation, so a number alone does not settle the question.\n\nSeek urgent medical care if a headache is sudden and reaches full intensity within about a minute, comes with fever and a stiff neck, follows a head injury, or brings new weakness, numbness, confusion, visual loss, or trouble speaking that does not go away. Also seek prompt assessment if a headache starts after age 50 as a new type, or gets steadily worse over days or weeks. (Sources: *Migraine* condition note and *Sudden severe headache and other dangerous headache patterns*.)\n\nTo decide whether preventive treatment makes sense for you, a clinician who can examine you and review your history is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 10284.32163600155,
          "red_flag_matched": false,
          "red_flag_matched_raw": false,
          "red_flag_negation_suppressed": false,
